In embedded systems:
	volatile tells the compiler that the value can change outside the program’s control.

Outside control means:
	Hardware register
	Interrupt Service Routine (ISR)
	DMA controller
	Memory-mapped peripheral
	Another core
	External device

when volatile used compiler must:
👉 Read from memory every iteration
👉 Not cache in CPU register


Volatile does NOT:
	❌ Make code thread-safe
	❌ Provide atomicity
	❌ Provide memory ordering
	❌ Prevent race conditions
	❌ Replace mutex
	❌ Replace std::atomic

Feature	            volatile	std::atomic
Prevent optimization	✅      	✅
Atomic operation	    ❌      	✅
Memory ordering			❌			✅
Thread-safe				❌			✅
Used for hardware registers	✅		❌

Embedded rule:
	👉 volatile for hardware
	👉 atomic for multithreading


Why Not Mark Everything Volatile?

Because:
	It disables optimizations
	Slows down code
	Increases memory traffic
	Not needed for normal variables